  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1)\n\nWell above normal temperatures are expected today with highs in the\n80s. These values are near record values for the day. The record \nvalue for today at CVG is 88 set in 1928, CMH is 85 set in 1969, and \nDAY is 86 set in 1897 and 1975. The current forecast high \ntemperatures are CVG 86, CMH 85, and DAY 86. \n\nWent with the NBM dry forecast for the daytime hours today given dry\nlow level air, however cannot rule out a few sprinkles.\n\nKey Message 2)\n\nAn active weather pattern will be in place for Wednesday through\nFriday. First there will be the potential for an isolated severe \nweather Wednesday afternoon into Wednesday evening. Damaging winds \nand hail will be the primary threats and generally south of \nInterstate 70. \n\nThe severe threat will transition into more of a heavy rain threat \ninto Wednesday night. With the multiple rounds of rain from Wednesday\nduring the day to Wednesday night, there will be some flooding \nconcerns. There is a convergent signal for an axis of west to east \nstorms with a focus generally a little north of the Ohio River to a \nlittle south of the Ohio River. There is some uncertainty in where \nthis axis of heavier rainfall will be possible. \n\nAdditional showers and storms will move into the area later on\nThursday and into Friday in advance and with a cold front. There is\nan additional severe threat with damaging winds the primary threat\nThursday into Thursday night. Gusty winds will be present for Friday\nwith wind gusts likely in excess of 30mph and a few gusts upwards of\n40 mph possible.\n\nFor temperatures during this time period, decreased temperatures\nslightly north of Interstate 70 on Wednesday due to earlier timing of\nclouds and storms there. On Thursday again decreased temperatures,\nprimarily north of the Ohio River. Updated temperatures Thursday\nnight and Friday to account for a sharper temperature trace with the\nstrong system moving through with temperatures falling during the \nday on Friday.\n\nKey Message 3)\n\nAfter the cold front passes on Friday, cooler air will work in for\nthe weekend. There is still some model variation on how cold\novernight lows will be, however there will be the potential for\nfrost.",
